[h1]Build All: Bulk Construction, One Click[/h1]

[b]Pick a building. Press one button. It is queued in every state you can build it in.[/b]

[hr][/hr]

[i]>>> IMAGE 1 (the GIF): press the button, the queue floods. [img]https://i.imgur.com/XXXX.gif[/img] <<<[/i]

[h2]You just finished Electricity[/h2]

Now every state in your empire wants a Power Plant, and the ones you already have want another level. You open the construction panel, and there are ninety states in the list.

So you click [b]+[/b]. Ninety times. Then you do it again next decade when coal power gives way to hydro, and again when your states outgrow what you built.

That is the entire reason this mod exists. It is the same ninety clicks — the game just does the clicking.

[i](It is not only power plants. Tooling Workshops after an industry tech, Railways across a new conquest, Urban Centres when the cities start choking — any building, any time you find yourself clicking the same [/i][b]+[/b][i] down a long list.)[/i]

[i]>>> IMAGE 2 (hero still): the panel with the Build All row and a big count. [img]https://i.imgur.com/XXXX.png[/img] <<<[/i]

[h2]How it works[/h2]

The mod adds two things to the construction panel you already use — the one that opens when you pick a building and it asks [i]"Select in which State to build"[/i]:

[list]
[*][b]A level stepper[/b] — 1, 5 or 10 levels.
[*][b]One build button[/b], reading [i]"Queue 10 levels in up to 47 states"[/i].
[/list]

Three things about it are worth knowing, because they are what make it usable rather than a blunt instrument:

[b]1. It obeys the filters you already set.[/b]
The panel's own [b]Location[/b] selector — [i]All / Domestic / Abroad[/i] — decides where it acts. Set it to Domestic and it stays home. Set it to Abroad and it builds only in other people's states, where you have investment rights. The workforce filters narrow it further. There are no new controls to learn: whatever the list is showing is what the button acts on, which is also why the button tells you how many states that currently is.

[i]>>> IMAGE 3 (Domestic vs Abroad, side by side -- the count changes). <<<[/i]

[b]2. It builds in the order the list is showing.[/b]
Sort the panel by Earnings and the queue is built richest-first. Sort by workforce and it follows that instead. The button walks the same list you are looking at, top to bottom, so you choose the priority by choosing the sort.

[i]>>> IMAGE 4 (sorted by Earnings, and the queue in that same order). <<<[/i]

[b]3. It spreads levels evenly, not one state at a time.[/b]
Ask for 10 levels and every state gets its first level before any state gets its second. This matters more than it sounds: a bulk order is a long queue, and if you cancel it halfway — or a war arrives and you need the construction elsewhere — your empire is evenly covered instead of three provinces deep and the rest untouched.

[h2]It is not a cheat, and not an autobuilder[/h2]

This shelf is crowded with automation, so to be exact about it:

[list]
[*][b]It presses the game's own + button.[/b] Not a copy of it — literally the same call, once per state. Normal construction queue, normal cost, normal build time, normal funding.
[*][b]It cannot build anything you could not.[/b] The game refuses any state you could not click yourself. States already at their level cap, or where you have no right to build, are skipped automatically.
[*][b]It never acts on its own.[/b] Nothing happens until you press the button. No monthly tick, no background queueing, no AI.
[*][b]It does not touch your treasury[/b], construction points or build times — not to charge you, and not to discount you.
[/list]

If you would not call clicking [b]+[/b] ninety times cheating, this is not cheating. It is the same ninety clicks.

[h2]Why the button says "up to"[/h2]

N is how many states the panel is listing, and a few of those may refuse — usually because they are already at their maximum level for that building. The game gives mods no way to count only the buildable ones, so the button states a ceiling and the tooltip says what gets skipped, rather than showing a confident number that turns out to be wrong.

[h2]Languages[/h2]

Every language Victoria 3 supports: [b]English, French, German, Spanish, Português do Brasil, Polski, Русский, 简体中文, 日本語, 한국어, Türkçe[/b].

The words for [i]State[/i], [i]Building Level[/i] and [i]Construction Queue[/i] are taken from the game's own text in each language, so the button reads like the rest of the interface. They are not native-speaker translations, though — [b]if something reads badly in your language, post the correction and it goes in.[/b]

[i]>>> IMAGE 5 (the panel in Japanese, proving the language claim). <<<[/i]

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]Game version 1.13.[/b]
[*][b]Ironman and achievements: fine.[/b] Mods do not disable achievements in Victoria 3.
[*][b]Save-safe.[/b] Add it or remove it mid-campaign; it stores nothing in your save.
[*][b]Single-player.[/b] Not tested in multiplayer, and not marked multiplayer-synchronised.
[*][b]Light on conflicts, but not immune.[/b] It replaces one small interface definition rather than the whole construction panel, so it should sit alongside most things — but any other mod that changes that same panel will clash. If you use one, load this first and tell me what breaks.
[/list]

[h2]Feedback[/h2]

If something does not queue when you think it should, say which building and which filter you had set. That is usually enough to find it.

[i]A "Max" option — fill every state to its cap — is the most likely next addition. I left it out rather than guess at the right ceiling. If you want it, say so.[/i]
